WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.046
This briefing is Book 11 of the
Federal Modernization Workbook,

3
00:00:05.046 --> 00:00:06.824
Zero Trust and Assessment.

4
00:00:06.824 --> 00:00:10.987
It's about how an agency measures
its Zero Trust posture across every

5
00:00:10.987 --> 00:00:15.075
estate it runs, and then turns what
the tools find into a real program.

6
00:00:15.075 --> 00:00:16.450
We'll take four questions.

7
00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:19.250
Why is Zero Trust a whole-agency property?

8
00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:21.763
What do the mandates and
tools actually cover?

9
00:00:21.763 --> 00:00:23.162
Who owns the posture?

10
00:00:23.162 --> 00:00:24.500
And how do you assess it?

11
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.268
If you keep one idea, keep this
one: unify first, assess second,

12
00:00:29.268 --> 00:00:33.550
and treat every tool as
evidence, never as the verdict.

13
00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:38.625
The slides and notes carry every detail
and source; I'll stick to what matters.

14
00:00:38.625 --> 00:00:42.350
Let's start with why one score
has to cover the whole agency.

15
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.375
Picture the usual assessment calendar.

16
00:00:48.375 --> 00:00:51.900
The network team reviews the
boundary, keyed by address.

17
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:56.900
The cloud team runs each provider's
posture scan, keyed by resource tags.

18
00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:00.106
The virtualization team
works a hypervisor checklist,

19
00:01:00.106 --> 00:01:03.312
keyed by whatever the management
plane calls a machine.

20
00:01:03.312 --> 00:01:06.463
At year end, someone puts
the three reports behind

21
00:01:06.463 --> 00:01:09.537
one cover page and calls
it the agency's maturity.

22
00:01:09.537 --> 00:01:12.676
It's really three partial
assessments with one title.

23
00:01:12.676 --> 00:01:14.762
And the failure is architectural.

24
00:01:14.762 --> 00:01:19.857
One database server, running as a virtual
machine, on an agency subnet, backing

25
00:01:19.857 --> 00:01:25.112
up to a cloud bucket, can show up as three
unrelated objects scored by three tools.

26
00:01:25.112 --> 00:01:28.237
The guidance describes the
enterprise, not a tenant.

27
00:01:28.237 --> 00:01:32.243
So unify first, then assess,
and defend that unified plane,

28
00:01:32.243 --> 00:01:36.163
because it becomes a complete
map of the attack surface.

29
00:01:36.163 --> 00:01:38.860
Next, two incidents that show why.

30
00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:45.720
Two documented incidents
show that the whole-agency

31
00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.972
view is a security property,
not a reporting preference.

32
00:01:48.972 --> 00:01:55.067
In May and June 2023, an actor used a
Microsoft consumer signing key to forge tokens

33
00:01:55.067 --> 00:02:01.072
and read Exchange Online mail across many
organizations, in some cases for weeks.

34
00:02:01.072 --> 00:02:04.297
The tokens were valid to every
system that checked them.

35
00:02:04.297 --> 00:02:06.977
What caught it was a
custom alert one federal

36
00:02:06.977 --> 00:02:09.873
department had built on
mailbox logs it paid for.

37
00:02:09.873 --> 00:02:11.934
The second sits beneath the guest.

38
00:02:11.934 --> 00:02:16.521
ESXiArgs compromised thousands of
ESXi servers, and a later flaw handed

39
00:02:16.521 --> 00:02:21.260
full hypervisor control to anyone in
a directory group with the right name.

40
00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.822
Even one vendor's two tools don't add up.

41
00:02:23.822 --> 00:02:27.956
ScubaGear is CISA's, the Zero
Trust Assessment is Microsoft's,

42
00:02:27.956 --> 00:02:30.873
and neither sees past
Microsoft's platform.

43
00:02:30.873 --> 00:02:32.584
Tools are evidence inputs.

44
00:02:32.584 --> 00:02:34.748
The agency owns the verdict.

45
00:02:34.748 --> 00:02:37.460
Next, the principles behind that.

46
00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:44.822
Why do so many programs stall
at Initial or low Advanced?

47
00:02:44.822 --> 00:02:49.298
Because they read CISA's maturity
model as a five-pillar shopping list.

48
00:02:49.298 --> 00:02:53.440
But the model runs those pillars
through cross-cutting capabilities,

49
00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.154
and the one programs fund
least is Governance:

50
00:02:56.154 --> 00:02:59.797
defining and enforcing policy
within and across the pillars.

51
00:02:59.797 --> 00:03:02.803
Its Traditional stage
reads like a failure list,

52
00:03:02.803 --> 00:03:06.334
with siloed enforcement and
little correlation of logs.

53
00:03:06.334 --> 00:03:10.473
The book answers with five principles,
and none of them is a purchase.

54
00:03:10.473 --> 00:03:14.131
Identity is the anchor, but
it isn't self-authenticating,

55
00:03:14.131 --> 00:03:18.347
so sessions are re-evaluated and
the identity plane is defended.

56
00:03:18.347 --> 00:03:21.164
Privileged identity gets
controls of its own,

57
00:03:21.164 --> 00:03:25.611
with no standing admin assignments
outside tested emergency accounts.

58
00:03:25.611 --> 00:03:28.898
Evidence must be reproducible
and machine-readable.

59
00:03:28.898 --> 00:03:31.637
Governance enforces; a policy that only

60
00:03:31.637 --> 00:03:34.959
produces next quarter's
finding is just a report.

61
00:03:34.959 --> 00:03:38.123
And the cloud boundary
is modeled, gap by gap.

62
00:03:38.123 --> 00:03:40.930
Next, what the mandates actually say.

63
00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:48.105
Four documents get cited as if
they were one, and they're not.

64
00:03:48.105 --> 00:03:50.918
Executive Order 14028 is binding,

65
00:03:50.918 --> 00:03:56.043
and required each agency to plan
its Zero Trust architecture.

66
00:03:56.043 --> 00:04:02.202
OMB M-22-09 is binding too, and it's the
one that sets specific actions by pillar,

67
00:04:02.202 --> 00:04:06.530
like phishing-resistant MFA and
reliable device inventories.

68
00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:10.351
CISA's maturity model is
guidance; it says so itself,

69
00:04:10.351 --> 00:04:13.105
and CISA's page for it is now archived.

70
00:04:13.105 --> 00:04:17.905
NIST SP 800-207 supplies the architecture.

71
00:04:17.905 --> 00:04:22.383
M-22-09's deadlines have passed,
but it hasn't been rescinded,

72
00:04:22.383 --> 00:04:25.267
and a passed deadline
doesn't retire an action.

73
00:04:25.267 --> 00:04:31.068
One adjustment: its logging action pointed
to a memo that was rescinded in May 2026.

74
00:04:31.068 --> 00:04:32.893
And here's the point people miss.

75
00:04:32.893 --> 00:04:38.243
No federal document defines a Zero Trust
score, or requires a maturity stage.

76
00:04:38.243 --> 00:04:43.680
Next, the directive that
is concrete: BOD 25-01.

77
00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:51.542
BOD 25-01 is narrower
and much more concrete.

78
00:04:51.542 --> 00:04:55.222
CISA issued it in December
2024 for every production

79
00:04:55.222 --> 00:04:58.329
cloud tenant with a
finalized SCuBA baseline.

80
00:04:58.329 --> 00:05:00.087
It has six required actions,

81
00:05:00.087 --> 00:05:05.053
from inventorying tenants and deploying
the SCuBA tools, to implementing the

82
00:05:05.053 --> 00:05:09.178
mandatory policies and explaining
deviations with each report;

83
00:05:09.178 --> 00:05:10.630
the slide has the dates.

84
00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:17.530
The mandatory list is a subset of the
baselines: for Microsoft 365 it's 50 policies.

85
00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:24.055
Google Workspace is in scope now too,
with its policies due December 1, 2026.

86
00:05:24.055 --> 00:05:28.459
Scope is limited to software as a
service, so Azure subscriptions are out

87
00:05:28.459 --> 00:05:33.230
even when they share the tenant, and
on-premises Active Directory is untouched.

88
00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:36.755
Inventory, deployment and
explanations take days.

89
00:05:36.755 --> 00:05:39.408
Implementing the policies is architecture,

90
00:05:39.408 --> 00:05:42.205
and that's what the
directive really exists for.

91
00:05:42.205 --> 00:05:44.990
Next, the two tools that measure it.

92
00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:53.491
Agencies run two free, read-only
assessments of their Microsoft cloud,

93
00:05:53.491 --> 00:05:57.178
and often drop one because
both seem to cover identity.

94
00:05:57.178 --> 00:05:59.003
But they answer different questions.

95
00:05:59.003 --> 00:06:04.152
ScubaGear is CISA's tool, and asks whether
the tenant meets the federal baseline.

96
00:06:04.152 --> 00:06:07.189
Microsoft's Zero Trust
Assessment asks how mature

97
00:06:07.189 --> 00:06:10.153
the environment is against
Microsoft's guidance.

98
00:06:10.153 --> 00:06:13.818
It's a community project
maintained by Microsoft employees,

99
00:06:13.818 --> 00:06:17.264
not an official product, and
no support program covers it.

100
00:06:17.264 --> 00:06:20.915
They overlap on Entra ID
and some Defender policies.

101
00:06:20.915 --> 00:06:24.371
But only ScubaGear covers
Exchange Online, Teams,

102
00:06:24.371 --> 00:06:29.765
SharePoint and Power Platform, and 22 of
the 50 mandatory policies live there.

103
00:06:29.765 --> 00:06:33.434
Drop ScubaGear and you leave
mandatory policies unassessed,

104
00:06:33.434 --> 00:06:36.240
and stop the reporting
the directive requires.

105
00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.984
So run both, and reconcile
at the control level:

106
00:06:38.984 --> 00:06:44.690
the tenant setting, and the SP
800-53 control it supports.

107
00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:48.850
Next, everything beyond Microsoft 365.

108
00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:56.800
Everything so far stops at
the edge of Microsoft 365.

109
00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:01.484
Nothing on BOD 25-01's list
covers infrastructure accounts,

110
00:07:01.484 --> 00:07:04.450
the agency network or
the virtualized estate.

111
00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:08.149
So the whole-agency argument
rests on the Zero Trust documents,

112
00:07:08.149 --> 00:07:14.337
and on the SP 800-53 controls every
security plan already asserts.

113
00:07:14.337 --> 00:07:17.113
Three things get reconciled
before you assess.

114
00:07:17.113 --> 00:07:20.387
One inventory, with no
component counted twice.

115
00:07:20.387 --> 00:07:26.299
One naming authority, because each cloud
and VMware ships its own private DNS.

116
00:07:26.299 --> 00:07:30.208
And no implicit trust from any
boundary: a site-to-site VPN,

117
00:07:30.208 --> 00:07:35.612
a default virtual network and a flat
virtualized segment are the same mistake.

118
00:07:35.612 --> 00:07:39.718
The hypervisor is its own threat
class, so its management plane

119
00:07:39.718 --> 00:07:43.587
is assessed as its own surface,
not inferred from its guests.

120
00:07:43.587 --> 00:07:47.238
And every provider's tool answers
only for its own platform.

121
00:07:47.238 --> 00:07:49.630
Next, who owns the posture.

122
00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:55.418
Every pillar already has an owner.

123
00:07:55.418 --> 00:07:58.992
What most agencies lack is an
owner for the posture itself.

124
00:07:58.992 --> 00:08:03.569
M-22-09 already required a Zero
Trust implementation lead,

125
00:08:03.569 --> 00:08:06.904
and many agencies will give
this role to that person.

126
00:08:06.904 --> 00:08:11.468
The program lead owns the definition of
the whole-agency score, what it covers

127
00:08:11.468 --> 00:08:16.317
and what it leaves out, plus cross-pillar
correlation and the assessment calendar.

128
00:08:16.317 --> 00:08:19.335
The lead decides priority
but doesn't accept risk,

129
00:08:19.335 --> 00:08:24.118
and shouldn't also own the relationship
with the agency's largest cloud vendor.

130
00:08:24.118 --> 00:08:26.943
The CIO decides scope and sequence.

131
00:08:26.943 --> 00:08:30.517
The CISO sets the evidence
standard and approves the tools.

132
00:08:30.517 --> 00:08:33.618
The authorizing official
accepts residual risk.

133
00:08:33.618 --> 00:08:38.645
Exceptions split on time: one with an
expiration date is a CISO decision;

134
00:08:38.645 --> 00:08:42.331
one with no end date goes to
the authorizing official.

135
00:08:42.331 --> 00:08:47.018
And pillar owners decide how to fix a
finding, never that it doesn't need fixing.

136
00:08:47.018 --> 00:08:49.050
Next, how to get there.

137
00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:53.825
How do you get there?

138
00:08:53.825 --> 00:08:56.150
First, close before you assess.

139
00:08:56.150 --> 00:09:00.388
Many agencies met BOD
25-01's early dates and

140
00:09:00.388 --> 00:09:03.725
now treat ScubaGear
reporting as the answer.

141
00:09:03.725 --> 00:09:08.250
But ScubaGear measures deviation from
a baseline; it doesn't close any.

142
00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:12.300
A thermometer that reports a fever
every day isn't a treatment plan.

143
00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:16.779
So the program's six-phase sequence
puts the assessors at step five,

144
00:09:16.779 --> 00:09:21.503
as confirmation rather than discovery:
understand, design, implement,

145
00:09:21.503 --> 00:09:25.575
govern, validate, and then the
authorizing official decides.

146
00:09:25.575 --> 00:09:28.275
That's the Risk Management
Framework's own order.

147
00:09:28.275 --> 00:09:31.917
Before validating, build the
layers a finding depends on,

148
00:09:31.917 --> 00:09:36.488
like naming, certificates, workload
identity and governed exceptions.

149
00:09:36.488 --> 00:09:41.908
Meanwhile, route every current finding
to exactly one response: a configuration

150
00:09:41.908 --> 00:09:46.925
change now, architectural work with an
owner and a date, or a risk acceptance.

151
00:09:46.925 --> 00:09:49.925
A finding with no response
is the only failure.

152
00:09:49.925 --> 00:09:52.210
Next, running the assessment.

153
00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:58.960
Then run the assessment,
and keep the evidence clean.

154
00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:02.985
A few things are decided before either
tool touches a production tenant.

155
00:10:02.985 --> 00:10:07.435
Scope comes from the BOD
25-01 tenant inventory.

156
00:10:07.435 --> 00:10:10.912
Each tool gets its own dedicated
host inside the boundary,

157
00:10:10.912 --> 00:10:14.823
because their dependencies conflict,
and its own reader account.

158
00:10:14.823 --> 00:10:17.088
Output is handled as controlled data,

159
00:10:17.088 --> 00:10:20.961
and the assessment accounts belong
in the agency's logging plan.

160
00:10:20.961 --> 00:10:24.991
For ScubaGear, the configuration
file is the exception record:

161
00:10:24.991 --> 00:10:28.247
omissions with a rationale
and an expiration date.

162
00:10:28.247 --> 00:10:31.580
It's versioned, and its hash
is recorded with every run,

163
00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:33.322
along with the tool version.

164
00:10:33.322 --> 00:10:38.398
The results file is never renamed
or edited, only hashed and copied.

165
00:10:38.398 --> 00:10:41.785
The Zero Trust Assessment can
take more than a day to run.

166
00:10:41.785 --> 00:10:46.496
And one caution: install the program's
toolkit only from its own repository,

167
00:10:46.496 --> 00:10:50.523
because a public Python package
with the same name is unrelated.

168
00:10:50.523 --> 00:10:53.110
Next, ingesting the evidence.

169
00:10:56.860 --> 00:11:00.609
Most agencies run ScubaGear,
save the HTML report,

170
00:11:00.609 --> 00:11:03.260
and reopen it when an assessor asks.

171
00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:08.653
The program treats each tool as an adapter
instead: one input to one evidence chain,

172
00:11:08.653 --> 00:11:12.460
with the tools left exactly as
their publishers ship them.

173
00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:14.873
Each run flows through four stages.

174
00:11:14.873 --> 00:11:16.810
The tool writes its raw output.

175
00:11:16.810 --> 00:11:22.347
Each result becomes a claim, stamped with
who issued it, when, and a lineage hash.

176
00:11:22.347 --> 00:11:25.473
Claims from one interval
are sealed into a bundle.

177
00:11:25.473 --> 00:11:29.861
And the bundle emits OSCAL
assessment results and POA&M items.

178
00:11:29.861 --> 00:11:34.548
Take one example: the policy that
blocks legacy authentication fails.

179
00:11:34.548 --> 00:11:38.985
The claim ties back to the raw result,
and the bundle opens a POA&M item.

180
00:11:38.985 --> 00:11:42.155
Months later, an assessor
can rebuild the bundle and

181
00:11:42.155 --> 00:11:45.547
walk back to the raw output
without re-running anything.

182
00:11:45.547 --> 00:11:48.709
The limits are stated too:
it runs on an interval,

183
00:11:48.709 --> 00:11:51.060
and Google Workspace is still a gap.

184
00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:54.210
Next, turning findings into a program.

185
00:11:57.960 --> 00:12:00.598
Last, turn findings into a program.

186
00:12:00.598 --> 00:12:02.935
Read each result before counting it.

187
00:12:02.935 --> 00:12:05.460
Only failures and warnings are findings.

188
00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:08.460
A skipped check isn't
a gap and isn't a pass.

189
00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:11.509
It's absence of evidence,
recorded with the reason,

190
00:12:11.509 --> 00:12:15.598
often a license or a government-cloud
feature that isn't available.

191
00:12:15.598 --> 00:12:18.605
Findings are scored with
a documented method,

192
00:12:18.605 --> 00:12:23.748
priority weighs severity against effort,
and the buckets run from P1 down to P4.

193
00:12:23.748 --> 00:12:26.489
Quick wins go into the next change window;

194
00:12:26.489 --> 00:12:29.472
major projects go on a
pillar owner's roadmap.

195
00:12:29.472 --> 00:12:35.871
Both tools' findings are reconciled
against the same SP 800-53 control,

196
00:12:35.871 --> 00:12:39.247
so two pieces of evidence become
one finding for one owner.

197
00:12:39.247 --> 00:12:43.753
Anything not fixed and not excepted
becomes a POA&M item the authorizing

198
00:12:43.753 --> 00:12:47.884
official approves, and drift goes
to the authorizing official too.

199
00:12:47.884 --> 00:12:49.964
Then re-run after every wave,

200
00:12:49.964 --> 00:12:54.122
so a finding is closed by
evidence, not by a ticket status.

201
00:12:54.122 --> 00:12:56.310
Let's close with the decisions.

202
00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:01.435
So, four decisions.

203
00:13:01.435 --> 00:13:04.597
Name an agency-level owner
of the Zero Trust posture,

204
00:13:04.597 --> 00:13:07.685
separate from every cloud
provider relationship.

205
00:13:07.685 --> 00:13:11.415
Reconcile the network, every
cloud and the virtualization

206
00:13:11.415 --> 00:13:15.373
estate to one authoritative record
before scoring any of them.

207
00:13:15.373 --> 00:13:21.777
Close the BOD 25-01 mandatory
findings before measuring maturity,

208
00:13:21.777 --> 00:13:25.173
with Google Workspace
due December 1, 2026.

209
00:13:25.173 --> 00:13:29.153
And move every exception into
the versioned configuration file,

210
00:13:29.153 --> 00:13:33.723
with an expiration, routing each standing
one to the authorizing official.

211
00:13:33.723 --> 00:13:35.922
The next step needs no procurement.

212
00:13:35.922 --> 00:13:40.015
Find out, pillar by pillar, which
evidence can be re-run without

213
00:13:40.015 --> 00:13:44.348
the operator's help, because every
no marks the adoption boundary.

214
00:13:44.348 --> 00:13:48.059
And put the assessment hosts and
accounts in the logging plan now.

215
00:13:48.059 --> 00:13:49.736
Unify first, assess second,

216
00:13:49.736 --> 00:13:53.698
and let named people make the
decisions the evidence supports.

217
00:13:53.698 --> 00:13:57.310
Book 11 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

218
00:13:57.310 --> 00:13:58.140
Thank you.
